    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d171">

                <pb facs="0345" n="255" sameAs="frus:frus1932v02/pg255"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.5131/908: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France (Marriner) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-11-08T12:00:00+00:00">November 8,
                        1932—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received November 8—9:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>640. My 634, November 2, 3 p.m.<anchor n="67" xml:id="d171-f1"/> I am pleased to
                    inform you that after considerable effort on the Embassy’s part, and provided
                    the United States Treasury regulation regarding the free admission of dress
                    samples is issued on the same day, the Ministries of Foreign Affairs and
                    Commerce have agreed to the issuance of the copper decree. The consent of the
                    French Ministry of Finance is still required and its answer is expected today or
                    tomorrow. Is this arrangement agreeable to you and how many days’ notice would
                    you require?</p>
                <p>It is understood that the Treasury regulation will render the provision 3 of
                    section 308 of the Tariff Act applicable to samples of wearing apparel.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Marriner</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
